{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation pushed for review: Gerrit change 2556 — https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2556 (commit afe5ecf43bc0, branch reb-3a02-mcp-schema).\n\nThe 41-property list was verified authoritatively rather than trusted: derived by running find_drift() with BASELINED_OMISSIONS monkeypatched empty. It matched exactly, no differences.\n\n## AC1 — declared 39 of 41; 2 are permissive omissions WITH stated reasons (AC3)\n\n- GateResultOut: all 5 declared (criteria_count, line_count, keyword_count, ac_items, file_impact).\n- TicketStateOut: all 23 declared.\n- NextBatchOut: 11 of 12. `tasks` is omitted with a reason — it belongs to the CLI-only `next-batch --limit=0` reduced variant (_engine_support/next_batch.py:559); the library/MCP path next_batch_state -> to_json_dict emits `batch` and never `tasks`.\n- CreateResultOut: 0 of 1. `title` is omitted with a reason — only the CLI's `create --output json` emits it (_commands/composer.py:481); every MCP create tool returns rebar.create_ticket(return_alias=True), which builds {id, alias, description_warning, duplicate_warning} and drops title. Declaring it would advertise a permanently-null title on a ticket that always has one, and which the caller supplied as the tool argument.\n\n## AC2 — BASELINED_OMISSIONS DELETED, not emptied\n\nThe dict, its comment block, and its merge line in find_drift are all removed. Grepping the gate for both `BASELINED_OMISSIONS` and `3a02` returns ZERO hits. `check_mcp_models --check` reports 'no missing declarations'.\n\n## The non-obvious hazard this surfaced\n\nDeclaring an optional pydantic field makes it EMIT — and 15 of the newly declared properties have a canonical type admitting NO null (const: true, bare string, enum $ref, integer). A naive declaration therefore made real payloads VIOLATE THEIR OWN SCHEMA; 8 contract tests caught it (test_schema_ticket_state[mcp] and six test_canonical_tool_result_validates cases).\n\nFix: a new `_OmitUnsetOut` base with a model_serializer(mode='wrap') that pops a per-class _omit_when_unset tuple when the field was never set, modelled on the existing _HealthOut. **The emitted bytes are unchanged** while the declaration still appears in outputSchema. _HealthOut itself was deliberately NOT reused, because it would have suppressed the 9 already-declared defaults that _mcp_budget._wire_bytes documents as re-added.\n\nTimestamps are typed `int | str | None`, never bare int: _mcp_errors.js_safe_result's docstring warns an int-declared field is coerced back to a lossy bare number on FastMCP re-validation (bug 6fe7). A regression test pins this.\n\n## AC4 — wire-contract impact assessment\n\n**All 39 newly declared fields were ALREADY on the wire** as undeclared extra='allow' pass-throughs. None is genuinely new data, so the change DOCUMENTS AN EXISTING SHAPE rather than altering it. Evidence: rebar.show_ticket() against this store's real ticket 3a02 returns 12 of the TicketStateOut fields today, and a union over 200 closed tickets adds 5 more; the remaining 6 are conditional reducer projections (_processors.py:121,186; _io/_provenance.py:56-58) that flow through extra='allow' identically whenever set. js_safe_result's docstring independently confirms it: 'TicketStateOut declares NO timestamp fields ... so created_at/updated_at pass through undeclared'. GateResultOut's 5 are returned on every return path of check_ac_compute / quality_check_compute (_engine_support/gates.py:64-79, 316-360). NextBatchOut's 11 are emitted unconditionally by to_json_dict (next_batch.py:331-373).\n\nOne caveat for the record: GateResultOut is ONE model serving TWO tools, so check_ac's outputSchema now advertises line_count etc. that check_ac never returns, and vice versa. The canonical gate_result.schema.json already had that shape, and _OmitUnsetOut keeps the runtime payloads disjoint exactly as before.\n\n## A test that contradicted this ticket\n\nF5 had added `test_the_baseline_names_its_tracking_ticket_and_is_not_open_ended`, asserting `'3a02-66ea-9229-470c' in source` — the exact OPPOSITE of AC2, which requires the baseline be deleted. It was removed and replaced with four tests: the baseline is gone rather than emptied, the four models cover their schemas minus only the documented omissions, every permissive omission is preceded by a reason comment, and the null-less-when-unset plus JS-safe-timestamp behaviours.\n\n## Verification\n\ncheck_mcp_models --check OK; drift heldout suite 17 passed; `pytest tests -k mcp` 801 passed / 7 skipped / 0 failed; schema+budget+model+output sweep 1007 passed; make lint exit 0 (complexity new=0 increased=0, mechanism new=0 increased=0); make typecheck Success across 742 files. _mcp_models.py is 616 lines (was 508) against the 800 cap — 184 to spare, and no src/rebar file exceeds the cap."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788407488990225001,"uuid":"11eec734-88c6-4653-bea2-6c425176bd24"}